{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: forecasts are consistently above realized prices across all examples; apply a downward level shift (roughly 5–10 EUR) to the entire path, with larger cuts when the last-30d history is in the 80+ range.",
    "Trend/turning-point lag: when the market is rolling over (history peaks then starts falling), the model continues to project flat-to-higher prices; add a regime check—if recent momentum is negative, force a declining path rather than stabilization.",
    "Overshoot after high-price regimes: following spikes/plateaus near 82–84, the model predicts re-acceleration back toward highs, but outcomes mean-revert lower; cap upside and impose mean reversion toward a lower anchor (≈70–73) when prior month traded >80.",
    "Horizon-dependent error growth: the overprediction widens with forecast horizon (later days are most inflated); increase the downward adjustment with lead time (small cut in week 1, larger cut by weeks 3–4).",
    "Volatility miscalibration: the model’s day-to-day swings are too optimistic on the upside and miss sharp drawdowns; when recent realized volatility is elevated and direction is down, widen downside moves (fatter left tail) and dampen rebound amplitude."
  ]
}
